<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Records of the Three Kingdoms</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sanguo zhi, Sanguozhi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Records of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> (Sanguo zhi) is the official history of the Three Kingdoms period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> (220–280 CE), written by Chen Shou in the late third century. Its accounts of the states of Wei, Shu and Wu, expanded by Pei Songzhi's fifth-century commentary, are the main source for the period and for the novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a>. Its chapter on the eastern peoples is the earliest detailed description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms.jpg" alt="Records of the Three Kingdoms" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 猫猫的日记本 (Cat's diary) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Fragment_of_Biography_of_Bu_Zhi_History_Books_of_Three_Kingdoms_01_2012-12.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the Chinese <b>Sanguozhi</b>, kingship passed early on from the Sono clan to the Gyeru, whose kings bore the royal surname Go.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Sanguozhi</b> records that an envoy of Bodiao, king of the Da Yuezhi, usually identified with Vasudeva, reached the Wei court in 229, the last significant Kushan contact with China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Hou Han shu and the account of the Wuhuan and Xianbei in the <b>Sanguo zhi</b>, the survivors split into two groups named after the mountains in which they took refuge: the Wuhuan and the Xianbei.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Wei zhi, part of the <b>Records of the Three Kingdoms</b>, Queen Himiko took the throne of Wa as a spiritual leader after a major civil war, while her younger brother handled the affairs of state, including relations with the court of the Kingdom of Wei.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yayoi-culture">Yayoi culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chinese <b>Records of the Three Kingdoms</b> describe how the Wa people, after years of war in the late 2nd century, agreed to make Himiko their ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/yamatai">Yamatai</a></li></ul></section>
